description = "Many tall-folk consider goblins, their traditions, and ponygirls in particular to be primitive. However, the equipment used by goblin riders is remarkably sophisticated. Many features that appear crude, accidental, or merely perverse are carefully designed to serve a specific purpose.

[b]Boots[/b]
Goblins consider boots the most important part of a ponygirl’s equipment. This is reflected in the many goblin sayings about ponygirls and their boots, such as: “Happy feet, happy pony.”
Ponyboots, also known as hoof-boots, are made from heavy-duty leather. They usually reach to the knees, though some extend above them.
Each boot is fitted by hand and regularly adjusted. The leather is usually applied while slightly damp and then tightly laced. As it dries, it shrinks slightly and moulds itself to the ponygirl’s calves. This keeps the boots tight and stable while preventing chafing.
The sole is thick and rigid, usually made from layered leather reinforced with iron. It is shaped like a horse’s hoof, forcing the ponygirl to stand and walk on the balls of her feet. A broad horseshoe protects the sole from wear and provides traction on rough ground.
Despite its crude appearance, the curved design distributes the ponygirl’s weight evenly, prevents her foot from twisting inside the boot, and provides a remarkably secure and stable stance.

[b]Harness[/b]
A tightly fitted leather harness around the torso is another essential piece of equipment. Dozens of straps support the breasts while spreading and lifting the buttocks. The latter is largely an aesthetic choice, but one common among most goblin tribes.
The harness’s primary function lies in the many steel rings distributed across it. These allow bags, saddles, traces, and even small vehicles to be attached to the ponygirl with ease.
A tight fit is paramount. Though often uncomfortable for young fillies, a properly fitted harness prevents chafing, distributes weight evenly, and protects the she-beast’s long-term health.
Some harnesses are complemented by an underbust corset. This improves posture and provides additional support for the ponygirl’s breasts, particularly if they are notably large.

[b]Collar[/b]
A posture collar, also known as a neck corset, is in many ways the psychological anchor of the gear. It enforces a pleasing posture, helps the ponygirl remain focused, and prevents her from gawking around.
The collar also has a less obvious psychological effect. Unable to turn her head and see what is happening beside or behind her, the ponygirl soon grows wary of possible punishment and consequently becomes more attentive and obedient.
A steel ring at the front of the collar provides a convenient place to attach a leash when needed.

[b]Headharness[/b]
A head harness is another essential piece of equipment. Leather straps encircle the ponygirl’s head, primarily to hold the gag in place. The gag is usually a simple, rounded wooden bar placed in her mouth and pulled back until it presses painfully into its corners.
The harness and gag also prevent intelligible speech. Their primary purpose, however, is to hold the reins, allowing the ponygirl to be controlled from a saddle or vehicle behind her, or while being led on foot.
Even the slightest tug pulls painfully at the gag. As a result, even the most rebellious ponygirl finds it difficult to resist a firm pull for long.
Whenever possible, the gag is wrapped in cloth soaked in goblin cum. This keeps the ponygirl drugged for longer periods.
Some goblins also fit the head harness with blinders. These restrict the ponygirl’s vision and amplify the effects of the neck corset. While the ponygirl is standing or resting, the blinders can be folded over her eyes, depriving her of sight entirely.
Blinders are most commonly used as punishment or when entering tall-folk settlements. In the latter case, they prevent the ponygirl from making eye contact with free people. This is believed to discourage rebellious thoughts while making it easier for passers-by to ignore her condition.

[b]Armbinder[/b]
The final piece of conventional bondage is the armbinder. Goblin tribes often differ in which style they prefer. Designs range from a simple monoglove and the more flexible box binder to an almost completely immobilising leather pouch that holds the arms in a reverse-prayer position.
The chosen armbinder also determines what kind of saddle is used. In some designs, the ponygirl’s bound arms are carefully padded and serve as a seat for the rider. In others, a dedicated saddle is attached to her harness or directly to the armbinder.
Regardless of their design, all armbinders are remarkably sturdy and virtually impossible to break without tools.

— Excerpt from “Ponygirls of the Borderlands: An Essay on Goblins and Their Mounts,” by Scholar Lula of Trisa Academy"
